<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byblos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jbeil, Jubail, Gibelet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Byblos is an ancient port city on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, known in Arabic as Jbeil or Jubail. Inhabited for some 7,000 years, it was a leading Phoenician city that traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the Greek word for book derives from its name. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> it was the lordship of Gibelet, held by the Genoese Embriaco family within the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byblos.jpg" alt="Byblos" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orient at de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byblos_Libanon_2003.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
